In all cases we observe the formation of cell density aggregations, where the height and width of peaks varies with the choice of phenotypic switching functions. These results support the idea that phenotypic switching naturally curbs the density of aggregates. Generally, the chemotactic phenotype forms a concentrated group at the core of the aggregate, while the secreting phenotype is more diffusely spread about the centre.Fig. 2
